*Quoted text:** \u201c\\(k=0\\): Vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots,x=n\\) cover all points.\u201d  \n   - **Analysis:**  \n     * Each vertical line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\).  \n     * The collection of all \\(n\\) vertical lines therefore covers every point of the triangle.  \n     * All lines are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is indeed realizable.  \n   - **Conclusion:** Correct and fully justified.\n\n6. **Construction for \\(k=1\\)**  \n   - **Quoted text:** \u201c\\(k=1\\): Verticals \\(x=1,\\dots,x=n-1\\) plus sunny line through \\((n,1)\\).\u201d  \n   - **Analysis:**  \n     * The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines cover all points with \\(x\\le n-1\\).  \n     * The remaining point \\((n,1)\\) is covered by a single sunny line; any line not parallel to the axes or to \\(x+y=0\\) passing through \\((n,1)\\) suffices (e.g. \\(y=-\\frac12(x-n)+1\\)).  \n     * Exactly one sunny line is used, so \\(k=1\\) is realizable.  \n   - **Conclusion:** Correct and fully justified.\n\n7. **Construction for \\(k=3\\)**  \n   - **Quoted text:** \u201c\\(k=3\\): Three sunny lines cover the 6 rightmost points, verticals cover the rest.\u201d  \n   - **Analysis:**  \n     * The six rightmost points are \\((n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\).  \n     * The solution does **not** specify the three sunny lines. A standard choice is:  \n       - \\(L_1:\\) through \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) (slope\u202f1).  \n       - \\(L_2:\\) through \\((n-2,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n       - \\(L_3:\\) through \\((n-2,3)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n       Each line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), hence is sunny, and together they cover all six points.  \n     * The remaining points (those with \\(x\\le n-3\\)) are covered by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots,x=n-3\\).  \n     * The solution\u2019s statement that \u201cthree sunny lines cover the 6 rightmost points\u201d is therefore true, but the lack of an explicit description and verification constitutes a presentation gap.  \n   - **Conclusion:** The construction is correct, but the proof is incomplete \u2192 **Justification Gap**.\n\n8. **General claim about the constructions**  \n   - **Quoted text:** \u201cAll constructions work by the pigeonhole principle and coverage analysis.\u201d  \n   - **Analysis:**  \n     * The phrase is vague; it does not replace the need for explicit verification of each construction. While the pigeonhole principle can be used to argue that a certain number of lines is sufficient, the solution does not spell out the argument.  \n   - **Conclusion:** The statement does not constitute a rigorous justification \u2192 **Justification Gap**.\n\n**Overall assessment:** The solution arrives at the correct set of admissible values \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) and provides valid constructions for each of those values. However, the upper\u2011bound part of the argument is incomplete: the necessity of a vertical line in column \\(x=n-2\\) is not proved, the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) is not fully justified, and no argument is given for why \\(k>3\\) cannot occur. Moreover, the construction for \\(k=3\\) is not explicitly described. These deficiencies are **Justification Gaps**, not critical logical errors, because the final answer is nevertheless correct."
